      "notes": "SC-004 requires every operation to carry a case for each configuration source the applicability rules permit for it. Teardown of a container built from a Dockerfile. The container is removed; the IMAGE the build produced is deliberately not asserted on, because `down` removes containers and leaving a built image behind is the correct half of 'what it removes and what it leaves' (FR-039)."
    },
    {
      "id": "case-down-malformed-config-rejected",
      "behaviors": [
        "bhv-readconfig-malformed-jsonc-rejected"
      ],
      "context": [],
      "scenarioContext": {
        "sdim-operation": "down",
        "sdim-config-source": "image",
        "sdim-container-state": "none",
        "sdim-features": "none",
        "sdim-layering": "single",
        "sdim-output-mode": "human"
      },
      "inputClass": "malformed",
      "oracleType": "spec-expectation",
      "resourceGroup": "docker-shared",
      "operations": [
        {
          "id": "op-down",
          "subcommand": "down",
          "argv": [
            "--workspace-folder",
            "${WORKSPACE}",
            "--remove"
          ],
          "fixtures": [
            "fx-errors-malformed-json"
          ],
          "expectFailurePhase": "config-resolution"
        }
      ],
      "expected": [
        {
          "channel": "chan-exit-code",
          "operation": "op-down",
          "assertion": {
            "nonZero": true
          }
        }
      ],
      "cleanup": {
        "containers": true,
        "images": false,
        "networks": true,
        "volumes": true,
        "tempdir": true
      },
      "notes": "FR-040 malformed class: `down` derives the resources to remove FROM the configuration, so an unparseable document leaves it with nothing to identify. Failing is the honest outcome; succeeding would claim a teardown that could not have been scoped."
    },
    {
      "id": "case-down-removes-container",
      "behaviors": [
        "bhv-down-removes-container"
      ],
      "context": [],
      "scenarioContext": {
        "sdim-operation": "down",
        "sdim-config-source": "image",
        "sdim-container-state": "running",
        "sdim-features": "none",
        "sdim-layering": "single",
        "sdim-output-mode": "human"
      },
      "inputClass": "valid",
      "oracleType": "spec-expectation",
      "resourceGroup": "docker-shared",
      "operations": [
        {
          "id": "op-up",
          "subcommand": "up",
          "argv": [
            "--workspace-folder",
            "${WORKSPACE}"
          ],
          "fixtures": [
            "fx-up-basic"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "op-down",
          "subcommand": "down",
          "argv": [
            "--workspace-folder",
            "${WORKSPACE}",
            "--remove"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "op-verify",
          "subcommand": "exec",
          "argv": [
            "--workspace-folder",
            "${WORKSPACE}",
            "--",
            "true"
          ],
          "expectFailurePhase": "exec"
        }
      ],
      "expected": [
        {
          "channel": "chan-exit-code",
          "operation": "op-down",
          "assertion": {
            "equals": 0
          }
        },
        {
          "channel": "chan-exit-code",
          "operation": "op-verify",
          "assertion": {
            "nonZero": true
          }
        }
      ],
      "cleanup": {
        "containers": true,
        "images": false,
        "networks": true,
        "volumes": true,
        "tempdir": true
      },
      "notes": "FR-039 teardown: after `down --remove` the workspace's container is GONE, which a `down` exit code cannot show — a teardown that removed nothing exits 0 too. The third operation execs into the container that should no longer exist and must fail; that failure is the evidence. (The container-graph channel cannot serve here: it reads an inspect the runner captures at an `up`/`exec` boundary, and after a successful removal there is nothing left to inspect.) The pinned reference has no `down` command, so this is evaluated against the declared expectation."
    },
